From 7c7ce6e0c8ce4670d6855ed72d3b9a4b6eed9ea0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 05:18:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Read a distributed prometheus query without CREATE TEMPORARY
 TABLE

The read rewrite ships timeSeriesSelector(...) inside cluster(view(...)) to every
shard, where it runs as the cluster entry's own user. cluster() and view() are
registered readonly; the selector was not, so ITableFunction::execute asked that
user for CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE before it read anything, and a cluster user
granted exactly what the shard read needs - SELECT on the shard-local TimeSeries
tables - failed every distributed PromQL read. Nothing preflighted that grant
either: it was asked for only when the cluster has a shard that is this server
itself.

The selector only reads, and asks for SELECT on the TimeSeries table itself, so
it is registered readonly as merge() and mergeTreeIndex() are, and the local-shard
preflight of that grant goes with it. The prometheusQuery/prometheusQueryRange
table functions still need it for themselves, and still ask for it before the
shard probe can report anything.

The cluster user of the restricted two-shard entry now holds only the two shard
databases, so the module's reads over that connection are regressions in
themselves, with a test that asserts the premise and reads back what it wrote in
SQL and over HTTP; a caller of the query endpoints without the grant now reads as
any other caller does.

